{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786428923,"nonce":"FAqFwoc7PD/Y/WrGKsBFfCPdQ5g=","message":"Fixed. invalid_label_value (B18) and annotation_unstorable (B19) are now first-class ingest_rejections.reason members, added by 00035 as a pure CHECK relaxation. The Down does NOT delete rows — ingest_rejections IS the evidence and its retention is already the shortest in the schema — it rewrites them to what version N would have recorded (undecodable) with the true reason preserved at the front of detail.\n\nThe storability rule is now binding in SPEC §L.3.2a, and it is deliberately TWO OPPOSITE VERDICTS from ONE predicate (alerts/domain.UnstorableReason): a label value is IDENTITY, so an unstorable one REJECTS the alert (rewriting a byte would file the observation under a key the upstream never sent — silently merging two Alerts is unrecoverable where losing one observation is not); an annotation is PROSE and explicitly not part of identity, so it is KEPT and sanitised to U+FFFD, matching b268f52's truncate-and-keep policy. Two refinements: an unstorable annotation NAME is dropped rather than sanitised (a jsonb key — two bad names would collapse to one and silently overwrite), and it is filtered before B7's count so a doomed annotation cannot evict a good one; sanitisation runs before B8 because U+FFFD is 3 bytes where the byte it replaces is 1.\n\nInvalid UTF-8 handled the same way. labels_test.go's \\xff corpus entry was INVERTED, not deleted, with a comment marking the deliberate reversal, and its slot filled by U+00FF.\n\n⚠️ Found and NOT fixed — filed separately: B18/B19 are unreachable through the push path, because Accept persists the raw body verbatim into ingest_batches.payload JSONB before per-alert bounds run.","files":null}]}